WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:05.150
One very important concept to understand with
these roles is that SQL has distinct levels

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.700
of roles for server tasks and database tasks.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:16.820
The roles you just saw, all being server-level
roles, do not give users access to individual

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:17.900
databases.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.170
For users to have permissions on databases,
those permissions either need to be granted

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:26.680
specifically or through one or more database
roles.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:36.370
I will expand the AdventureWorks2019 database
and then Security, Roles, and Database Roles.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:42.719
As we cover these roles, again, focus on the
purpose of roles, which is to control permissions

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:44.930
on, in this case, a database.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:52.070
Going from least restrictive to most restrictive,
let's start with the db_owner role, which

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:57.370
has complete control on a database, including
the ability to drop the database.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.859
Next, we have the db_securityadmin role, which
allows one to manage membership of custom

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:05.729
roles and manage overall permissions on a
database.

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:12.189
Thirdly, we have the db_accessadmin role,
which can add or remove access for Windows

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:17.180
accounts and groups and SQL accounts to and
from a database.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.400
Earlier I mentioned that the ability to restore
databases is a server role.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:28.020
For backups, however, the db_backupoperator
role is assigned to each database.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.680
The reasoning behind doing these roles on
these levels is: restores need to happen quickly,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:41.799
so a person in that role should be able to
restore any database on a server.

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:46.340
Backups happen on different schedules depending
upon the volume of data being backed up and

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.220
the needs for data to be as current as possible
should it need to be restored.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.409
The db_ddladmin role can run any data definition
language command, which includes CREATE, ALTER,

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:02.039
and DROP commands, the latter being the main
reason administrators need to be careful as

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.079
to who receives this role.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:11.200
The db_datawriter role allows one to add,
change, or delete data to, in, or from any

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.220
table in a database.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.980
The db_datareader role allows for viewing
any data in tables or filtered data through

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.080
views.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:27.430
There are two explicit deny roles that can
be placed on users in a database.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:34.440
The db_denydatawriter role prevents those
in that role from adding data to, deleting

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.330
data from, or modifying data in a database.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:45.140
The db_denydatareader role prevents one from
reading data in tables and views.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.550
Lastly, the public role for a database is
the same as that of the public role for the

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:55.800
server except, of course, the role is at the
database level and whatever permissions are

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.140
granted to the role apply to anyone with an
account assigned to the database.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:06.390
Before leaving this topic, the deny permissions
bring up an interesting point: why use them

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.280
when we can just keep people in the public
role?

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.319
The reason: these two roles ensure that certain
users can be prevented from ever reading data

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:21.510
or changing data even if a group they belong
to has access to a database.

